  "design_rule": "DESIGN v5 — CLAIM vs RESOLUTION (THREE classes).  (i) CLAIM/RESOLUTION SEPARATION: every stored field is classified — ASSERTED (a mutation MUST fail the guard), ASSERTED-VERDICT (the guard RE-MEASURES the live tree and asserts that the RECORDED verdict EQUALS the LIVE verdict — it does NOT assert that the verdict is TRUE), or RESOLUTION (informational drift, NEVER compared for equality).  A guard may FAIL only on a DURABLE claim — does the symbol still exist, is the count still above its floor, is the verdict still right; every derived field (line numbers, byte offsets, content hashes) is REPORTED as informational drift, never ASSERTED.  The row's own `said_kind` (line | hits | tests | bytes | presence | none) SELECTS which assertion applies to that row: `ok` is asserted on every row, and `said_ok` on every row EXCEPT a `line`-kind row (a locator drifts on every unrelated edit) and EXCEPT a row whose said_ok is None.  (ii) FLOOR SEMANTICS: a count_ge probe carries a FLOOR, never a freeze — a floor is a LOWER BOUND the live count must not fall below, so the tree may GROW without failing; a count_ge spec without an explicit positive integer floor RAISES (the previous default of 1 was vacuous: one hit passed, so the check could not fail), and a floor BELOW the measured live count is ALSO vacuous and is not acceptable; the count_ge verdict is the three-part predicate (a) live_count >= floor, (b) len(sidecar.files) >= floor, (c) every sidecar-named path still carries the marker — and live_files == sidecar.files is NOT compared (that is the treadmill).  (iii) REFRESHING IS A SEPARATE, NON-BLOCKING REGENERATION STEP: it rewrites the resolution fields and the timestamp and never decides whether a claim holds.  (iv) AGENTS.md §4's STALE-FLAG RULE: the PRODUCER stamps (`resolved_at`), the CONSUMER decides, a stamp older than 24 h means FAIL, and a missing, unparseable or future-dated stamp fails CLOSED.  Plus Wf-A269 §6.269.4h's generalised rule: a locator is a RESOLVER (symbol -> current line), not a FROZEN LINE NUMBER.  KNOWN LIMIT: the `hits` said-verdict is a LOWER BOUND (measured >= claimed), so it cannot certify a claim's BREAKDOWN.",
